<section class="case-sec" id="gap">
  <div class="reveal">
    <div class="sec-mark">§A — The gap the agent was built to close</div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Customer health inside Azure Storage lived across at least eight
        disconnected tools — telemetry in Azure Data Explorer, tickets in the incident system, customer-reported
        issues in a separate tracker, ownership graphs in yet another service.
        Every exec readout was a <strong>three-to-five day manual hunt</strong>: an analyst
        joined tables in their head, pieced together a narrative, and shipped a
        deck. By the time the story was told, the window to act on it was often
        closed.
      </p>
      <p>
        The ask from leadership wasn't "build us a chatbot." It was: <em>can anyone
        in this org, at any hour, ask a plain-English question about a customer
        and get a trustworthy answer?</em> The operative word is trustworthy.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="case-sec" id="architecture">
  <div class="reveal">
    <div class="sec-mark">§B — How the system is wired</div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        At the center: an agent on Azure OpenAI with function calling. Around it:
        a retrieval layer for metric definitions, ticket excerpts, and ownership
        records; a tool registry that executes live KQL against Azure Data
        Explorer; and an evaluation-and-guardrails layer that runs on every
        response before it ships.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
  {% include "partials/diagram-mcc.njk" %}
  <div class="prose reveal">
    <p>
      The non-obvious design choice was to treat <strong>retrieval as a tool, not a
      preprocessing step</strong>. The agent decides when it needs metric definitions
      versus raw rows versus ownership lookups, rather than receiving a
      pre-stuffed context window. That single decision is what lets it answer
      multi-hop questions like <em>"which APAC customers with &gt;10TB capacity have
      both ticket volume trending up and availability-loss regressions this quarter?"</em>
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="case-sec" id="prompt">
  <div class="reveal">
    <div class="sec-mark">§C — The system prompt</div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Every grounded behavior starts here. The shape of this prompt went
        through roughly forty revisions — every one scored by the eval harness
        before it shipped.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="code-panel panel reveal">
    <div class="cp-head"><span>// system-prompt.v7</span><span class="g">redacted · representative</span></div>
    <pre><code><span class="cm"># role</span>
You are a customer-health analyst for Azure Storage. You answer
questions by calling tools against live data. <span class="hl">You do not guess.</span>

<span class="cm"># grounding rules</span>
- Every numeric claim MUST cite the source (tool + query id).
- <span class="hl">If a tool returns zero rows, report that. Do not interpolate.</span>
- If you lack a needed tool or permission, say so and stop.
- <span class="hl">Never combine values from different time windows</span> without saying so.

<span class="cm"># tool selection policy</span>
1. Look up the customer by CustomerId before any other call.
2. Prefer aggregated tools (availability-loss and reliability summaries) over raw KQL.
3. Only write KQL when no aggregated tool can answer the question.
4. Budget: at most 6 tool calls per answer.

<span class="cm"># output schema</span>
Return a JSON object with: summary, findings[] (claim, evidence),
confidence (low|med|high), refusal (bool, reason).</code></pre>
  </div>
  <div class="prose reveal">
    <p>
      Two lines did disproportionate work. <em>"If a tool returns zero rows, report
      that"</em> killed a whole class of "I found no issues" hallucinations.
      <em>"Never combine values from different time windows"</em> killed a subtler one,
      where the model would happily mix last week's availability loss with last quarter's
      ticket volume.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="case-sec" id="kql">
  <div class="reveal">
    <div class="sec-mark">§D — A query the agent wrote</div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        For the long tail of questions no pre-built tool covered, the agent could
        write KQL. Every generated query passed a row-count assertion and a
        syntax check before its results were surfaced.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="code-panel panel reveal">
    <div class="cp-head"><span>// agent-generated KQL</span><span class="g">after grounding &amp; validation</span></div>
    <pre><code><span class="cm">// resolved from: "APAC customers >10TB w/ availability-loss regressions this Q"</span>
<span class="cm">// agent call: exec_kql(cluster="[analytics-cluster]", db="…")</span>
StorageAccountMetrics
| <span class="kw">where</span> TimeGenerated <span class="kw">between</span> (startofquarter(now()) .. now())
| <span class="kw">where</span> GeoRegion == "APAC"
| <span class="kw">summarize</span> CapacityTB = avg(CapacityBytes) / 1e12,
            WowLossMin = sum(AvailabilityLossMinutes) <span class="kw">by</span> CustomerId, AccountName
| <span class="kw">where</span> CapacityTB > 10
| <span class="kw">where</span> WowLossMin > tolong(prev(WowLossMin, 1, 0)) * 1.25
| <span class="kw">join</span> kind=inner TicketTrends <span class="kw">on</span> CustomerId
| <span class="kw">where</span> TicketsWoWDelta > 0
| <span class="kw">project</span> CustomerId, AccountName, CapacityTB, WowLossMin, TicketsWoWDelta
| <span class="kw">order by</span> WowLossMin desc
| <span class="kw">take</span> 25</code></pre>
  </div>
  <div class="prose reveal">
    <p>
      The user never saw the query first — they saw a paragraph with five
      customer names, then a link to the query, the row count, and the raw
      table. <strong>The query is the receipt.</strong> That's what turned "an LLM answered
      my question" into "a trustworthy signal I can act on."
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="case-sec" id="evals">
  <div class="reveal">
    <div class="sec-mark">§E — The eval harness came before the users</div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        A golden set of ~180 historical exec questions, each labeled with its
        grounded answer and expected citations. Every prompt change ran the full
        set before ship — the harness gates changes the way CI gates merges.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="table-panel panel reveal">
    <div class="cp-head"><span>// eval run — prompt v6 → v7</span><span class="g">180 cases · abridged</span></div>
    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table class="data-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Dimension</th><th>v6</th><th>v7</th><th>Delta</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td class="mono">groundedness (mean)</td><td class="num">0.841</td><td class="num">0.902</td><td class="num good">+0.061</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="mono">correctness (mean)</td><td class="num">0.793</td><td class="num">0.821</td><td class="num good">+0.028</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="mono">citation validity</td><td class="num">0.941</td><td class="num">0.827</td><td class="num warn">−0.114 ← regression</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="mono">refusal (appropriate)</td><td class="num">0.876</td><td class="num">0.902</td><td class="num good">+0.026</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="mono">refusal (over-refused)</td><td class="num">0.042</td><td class="num">0.058</td><td class="num warn">+0.016</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="mono">tool-budget exceeded</td><td class="num">0.033</td><td class="num">0.011</td><td class="num good">−0.022</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <div class="board-foot">// VERDICT: <span class="g">block ship</span> — citation validity regressed on 12% of cases. investigate tone-tweak before merge.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="prose reveal">
    <p>
      That regression is real: a prompt tweak that improved tone silently
      dropped citations on 12% of cases — the kind of thing no qualitative
      review would catch, and exactly the kind of thing that destroys user
      trust in production. The harness flagged it in under ten minutes.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="case-sec" id="changed">
  <div class="reveal">
    <div class="sec-mark">§F — What it changed</div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        The agent's own number is <strong>speed to trustworthy insight</strong>: questions
        that took a three-to-five day manual hunt now come back grounded in
        under two minutes, and <strong>8+ engineering and support teams</strong> converged on
        it as the single conversational surface for customer health.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="metric-row panel reveal">
    <div class="m"><span class="v">days → &lt;2 min</span><span class="d">customer-health time-to-insight — the agent's headline</span></div>
    <div class="m"><span class="v">13</span><span class="d">metric domains in the curated, governed catalog</span></div>
    <div class="m"><span class="v">8+</span><span class="d">teams unified on one conversational surface</span></div>
    <div class="m"><span class="v">~180</span><span class="d">golden-set cases gating every prompt &amp; model change</span></div>
  </div>
  <div class="prose reveal">
    <p>
      The agent serves a broader <strong>executive health-review program</strong> I drove across those
      same teams — a unified customer-health view that cut executive readiness
      insight from 3–5 days to near real-time. On the program's watch,
      <strong>&gt;10-day aging issues fell ~50%</strong> and <strong>time-to-mitigate improved
      ~16%</strong>; those are program outcomes with many hands on them, and the agent
      is the reason the insight arrived in time to matter. Churn risk was
      reversed for 10+ critical customers, each representing multi-millions in
      monthly Azure Storage revenue.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

